lating and  adapting your battle plan and knowing  the map layout makes the difference  between being the flanker or the flank  II but just as important as knowing  where your enemy is is knowing it ooh  your enemy is 20 unique operators will  populate Rainbow six siege and in the  closed alpha we got to play as 10 of  them though at first glance they look  like a bunch of jackbooted stormtroopers  they each have distinguishing attributes  that will allow sharp-eyed players to  gain valuable intel on what's coming  their way spot a guy with a classic  looking gas mask during the drone  scouting phase watch out for toxic gas  traps stuck to the walls is there a big  fella with a riot helmet your enemies  might have extra armor to absorb your  bullets and what about that hulking goon  with the big ol sledgehammer well you  can guess how that's going to go oh yeah  now that you know what's coming you can  take steps to neutralize your opponent's  strengths EMP grenades can keep those  gas traps from being remotely triggered  a heartbeat sensor can help you shoot  accurately through floors and ceilings  to shred that armor and reinforced  barricades will slow down even the  hammer in his tank in town tactics and  counter tactics actions and reactions  the core of Rainbow six siege lies in  this ever-evolving strategic battle  flanking outside the house to surprise  the attackers might work one round but  next time around they'll be ready an  electronic jammer might stymie your  buddies breach charge forcing you to  fire around from your breach charge  launcher players choose their operators  each round selecting which unique  abilities to bring into battle and which  to leave behind and adapting that  strategy from round to round these  choices throw a whole new crop of  considerations into the all-important  battle for Intel which leads us to the  question of how your team will share all  this Intel voice chat is pretty clearly  the best way and a communicative team is